{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>The first time</b><br>And your speech is lovely — [the word] מדברך is from the root of דיבור (speech)." "כפלח הרמון רקתך. Some say it is half a pomegranate, and the correct interpretation (in my eyes) is that it is the blossom that falls from the pomegranate tree when it begins to split open, like פולח and בוקע [in the earth]." "רקתך. Above the eye, at the edges of the temple." "מבעד. Like מבפנים (from within), like הבעד ערפל (judges [through a veil])." "צמתך. Some say it is the lock of hair that hangs down, like גלי צמתך." "<b>The second time</b><br>כחוט השני. Red and thin." "וכנגד המגנים — the necklace of the spot, that is, the bound ones." "<b>The third time</b><br>שפתותיך. They are the singers." "רקתך כפלח הרמון. כהניך — it is פעמון ורמון."], :book :song-of-songs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {1 "The bracketed [בארץ] appears in the Hebrew; its connection to the argument is unclear but translated literally.", 4 "Resolved י״א as יש אומרים (some say) based on context.", 6 "This line is obscure; translated hyper-literally without smoothing.", 7 "The identification of שפתותיך (your lips) with המשוררים (the singers) is left as Ibn Ezra states it, without explanation.", 8 "The phrase פעמון ורמון likely alludes to Exodus 28:33–34 (bells and pomegranates on the high priest's garments); left cryptic as Ibn Ezra gives no further explanation."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra's commentary on the Canticles" "http://beta.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH002030344/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:44:17.516365093Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}